When a tree is heavily pruned or topped, all the functional equilibrium ratios are highly disturbed. The Leaf to Fine Roots ratio drops, and the root/shoot ratio inflates. The tree has many more roots than needed by its reduced canopy.

To maintain alive its overnumbering roots, the tree needs C. The reduced canopy will not provide enough C for maintaining such a large root system. Usually, it is assumed that heavily pruned trees (and especially pollards) will loose a part of their rooting system that will die and decay.

If we do not take this into account, our pollarded trees in HisAFe will have such a large rooting system that they will use all the new C for above-ground growth, including unrealistic stem diameter growth. This will result in larger DBH in pollarded trees compared to un pollarded trees. This is what HisAFe predicts for pollarded walnut trees 

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/44842372/198564923-b0d288d2-ccd4-41da-a896-7e2498eedf7c.png)

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/44842372/198565114-a5e4290f-3421-48af-a8d8-77c19b910369.png)

We will use the Restinclières walnut pollards to check if this stimulated diameter growth is sensible or not. But in a first sight, it seems not to be sensible.

The main reason is probably that we need to incorporate in the model the impact of pruning and pollarding on root reduction.

